-- The scoped v1 of DESIGN.md's "Arrays: one resource each" tier-3 proof:
-- `write` and `read` touch the SAME mem at DIFFERENT, compile-time-
-- constant addresses, with NO `conflict_free` annotation -- unlike
-- conflict_free_mem.tr, which needs the annotation because its
-- addresses are runtime port values the compiler can't see disjoint.
-- Here the scheduler proves it on its own (schedule.rs's
-- `Exemption::Disjoint`) and drops the derived stall entirely.
--
-- Both rules are UNCONDITIONAL (fire every cycle), which makes this the
-- maximal-contention case: under the old, fully conservative model,
-- `read` (lower urgency) would NEVER fire at all, since `write` never
-- blocks or goes idle for it to catch a turn -- `read_count` would
-- stay 0 forever. See sim/mem_disjoint_rw_tb.v for the actual proof.
--
-- Bare integer literal addresses (`m[3]`, not `4'd3`) work here because
-- this feature also fixed a real, separate emission gap: a mem WRITE's
-- address used to compile unhinted (`mem_write_in_stmts`, writes.rs),
-- so a bare-literal write index hit "no concrete width for this
-- expression" even though a bare-literal READ index already worked
-- fine (`examples/checksum.tr`'s `m[0]`) -- fixed by hinting the
-- address compile with the mem's own address width, mirroring how the
-- data operand right next to it was already hinted.
module MemDisjointRw {
    mem m : [8][16]

    in x : [8]
    out y : [8] = 0
    out write_count : [8] = 0
    out read_count : [8] = 0

    rule write {
        m[3] := x
        write_count := write_count + 1
    }

    rule read {
        y := m[7]
        read_count := read_count + 1
    }

    schedule {
        urgency write > read
    }
}
